\chapter{Conclusion}
  \label{chap:conclusion}
  The objective of this study was to examine F\&C-speaking Creoles and Cajuns in Louisiana for signs of their racialization.
  One research question did so by asking whether subject pronouns vary according to these identities, and they did, suggesting that Creoles and Cajuns can be identified via their speech.
  This identification facilitates racialization.
  A second research question asked whether subject pronouns also vary according to how ethnically homophilous Creoles' and Cajuns' personal networks are, and they did not.
  However, this was likely because their personal networks were almost all highly homophilous, which is expected with racialized groups.
  Finally, a third research question asked if Creoles and Cajuns mark, police, and maintain boundaries around their identities in ways that are consistent with racialized groups, and they did.
  The overall picture presented by this study therefore suggests that Creoles and Cajuns are in fact racialized.

  The significant associations of Creoles with Blackness and Cajuns with Whiteness points to the idea that local Louisiana social categories have been swept into the broader racialized social system of the US through fractal recursivity \parencite{irvine_language_2000}.
  In other words, Creoles and Cajuns have not exactly been racialized on their own terms but by being made to fit into the broader system as the state has become less isolated.
  This phenomenon is not exactly a new finding in the sociological literature, especially for more marginalized groups such as Creoles, as there have been a number of examinations of how groups become Black, so to speak, when entering the US system \parencite{ducros_youre_2021, grosfoguel_race_2004, ifatunji_toward_2024}.
  However, this finding had not been established for Creoles and Cajuns even though the idea had been commonly repeated in the literature and even among residents in the form of describing Creoles as Black.
  What is perhaps more novel is that examinations of the incorporation of groups into the racialized social system of the US have typically involved immigrants, or at least intra-state migrants, rather than long-existing local ethnicities such as Creoles in Louisiana.
  In a sense, despite Louisiana's status as a US state for over 200 years, it has resembled a foreign territory relative to its neighboring states that the \foreign{Américains}, as effective foreigners to the state, are still colonizing.
  Additionally novel is the fact that this study did not focus purely on the racialization of the marginalized group but also of the higher status group in Cajuns who have been aligned with Whiteness.
  Accounting for such groups in a racialized social system is not completely new to sociology, either, although Whiteness has been less studied than the marginalized \parencite[\foreign{cf}.][]{bell_invisible_2021, brodkin_how_1998, carter_racialization_2012, ignatiev_how_1994, lewis_what_2004}.
  This study has thus injected Cajuns into the discussion on how Whiteness is constructed and expressed.

  In sociolinguistics more specifically, the present work fits into the recent movement towards incorporating sociological concepts of race into the field, often under the nomenclature of raciolinguistics \parencite{rosa_unsettling_2017}.
  Race is not a new topic in sociolinguistics, however, a field whose foundational work often focused on showing that the speech of marginalized groups is structured.
  This was in fact pursued to such a degree that an unintended side-effect was created in the promulgation of the idea that African-American speech constitutes a monolithic language variety.
  That idea has been unwound in recent decades, but how the social category of race has been conceptualized has remained by and large stubbornly couched in terms of bodies and color rather than in terms of how conflict and exploitation can lead to the racialization of any group.
  For variationist sociolinguistics, centered in the US, this situation is understandable: Bodies and color are almost inescapable in the racialized social system of the country.
  Indeed, Creoles and Cajuns are racialized categories today, and that racialization finds its clearest expression in how Creoles have become linked to Blackness and Cajuns to Whiteness despite these ethnicities each having very little to do with bodies initially, the former referring to anything native to the colonies and the latter to the descendants of the Acadians.
  The integration of Louisiana into the US, while it has been a slow process, is such that Creoles and Cajuns have been made to fit into the particular racialized borders permeating the the national psyche.

  Even today, Creoles and Cajuns can, for instance, be identified in their differing speech patterns in F\&C, as this study has shown.
  This is quantitatively observable but also obvious enough to locals that they explicitly comment on it, yet this does not appear to be the most salient delimiter for the two groups.
  When participants were asked what a Creole or a Cajun is, none began by explaining that Creoles are people who speak one way and Cajuns are people who speak another way.
  The ability to speak F\&C in any form surely took up a prominent place in categorizing alters as Cajun, but Cajuns using certain pronouns and Creoles using others only came up when participants were asked if the two groups speak F\&C differently.
  Notably, speaking F\&C was not used to categorize alters as Creole, by contrast, because their connection to Blackness has become so strong as to trump any other consideration.
  In general, it is the long historical process of linking Creoles to Blackness as well as the more recent process of linking Cajuns to Whiteness that was foregrounded in definitions of these groups, albeit very rarely was this done in explicit terms for the latter.

  Subject pronouns are an important marker of Creoles and Cajuns, nonetheless.
  Since the earliest grammatical descriptions of Louisiana Creole, what has at least been an implicit if not explicit decision has been made to describe the speech of those who use certain pronouns and to exclude those who do not.
  In a sense, this study helps to validate that approach in that Creoles themselves have a tendency towards a certain set of pronouns and Cajuns to a different set.
  Those sets neatly map onto what has been described as Creole and French, respectively, in the literature.
  Given that Creoles describe their language as Creole, at least when pushed beyond their use of French as the typical intial appellation, this creates a consistent picture of Creoles speaking Creole and that Creole having a specific structure.

  In another sense, this study also undermines that picture, though.
  Despite the tendency towards structural features that have been the hallmark of what the literature has called Creole, Creoles are not monolithic in their preference for those features.
  Some mix Cajun-marked and Creole-marked features even within a single paradigm, such as Gene's use of the Cajun-marked \lexi{je} for 1sg but also the Creole-marked \lexi{yé} for 3pl.
  These speakers, while they have been acknowledged to exist by researchers who focus on Louisiana, are nonetheless left out of descriptions of Creole because their speech is not seen as Creole enough.
  On the other hand, they are also left out of descriptions of French as their speech is likewise not seen as French enough.
  This study, however, not only acknowledges the existence of these speakers, as well as the fuzzy boundaries between what have been called French and Creole, but has offered a robust structural analysis of the whole range of F\&C subject pronouns without excluding those whose speech does not fit neatly into the boundaries of French or Creole that have been set out.
  The only other study to approach this sort of inclusion has been \textcite{klingler_language_2003}.
  More work can of course be done in this direction as well as in other directions towards which this study hints, though.

  \section{Limitations and future directions}
    Many of the future directions that the current research can take come from its limitations.
    A number of these are relevant to the quantitative analysis of subject pronouns, but others are relevant to the social network analysis and the discourse analysis, as well.
    None of these invalidate the work, but they do harken to the need for study replications as well as future directions that can flesh out what has already been found here.

    One overarching limitation is that the sample of participants was not a true random sample that could produce generalizable results.
    This is the primary case to be made for the need for replication studies.
    More specifically related to the results for subject pronouns, though, is that the broad US categories of Black and White may be even more predictive of subject pronoun usage than the local categories of Creole and Cajun.
    While this finding has been useful in and of itself, more can be done to understand the role that broad US racial categories have in predicting how subject pronouns vary between Creoles and Cajuns.
    There was a clear association of Cajuns with White and Creoles with non-White, but the US categories provided by participants were simplified due to the size of the sample of participants.
    Alternatively, as \textcite{giancarlo_dont_2019} did for Creoles of color, it would likely be fruitful to examine a large same of Creoles defined in any way and even of Cajuns.
    While Cajuns were more straightforwardly White in their self-identifications, they sometimes included \lexi{Cajun} itself as part of if not the whole of their racial self-identifications.
    Likewise, not all Creoles were ``of color'', in broad US terms, despite the category's strong association with Blackness.

    Creoles and Cajuns may also differ in their linguistic behavior from location to location, much like \citeauthor{rottet_language_1995}'s (\citeyear{rottet_language_1995}) results in Terrebonne-Lafourche hinted at for Indians and Cajuns.
    This could not be detected here because the sample of participants was not large enough to have a sufficient count of both Creoles and Cajuns in each of several towns nor even in each of several parishes.
    Such an analysis would likely have to use the level of towns as a delimiter rather than parishes as, anecdotally, there appeared to be differences even between neighboring towns.
    For example, Arnaudville seemed more likely to have speakers with Cajun-marked pronouns, whereas Cecilia, only seven miles southeast, was repeatedly described as having speakers of Creole.

    Gathering such a number of participants from several towns each would be a rather large task, however, likely requiring extensive funding.
    A more practical route for conducting a partial replication of the current study would be to limit the sample to many participants in a single town.
    The towns of St Martinville and Breaux Bridge would be ideal choices.
    The former is home to two Cajun-centric heritage sites, the Acadian Memorial and the Evangeline Oak, which are juxtaposed against an African-American museum that gives a central place to Creoles in its exhibits.
    St Martinville also plays up the presence of F\&C with significant French -- but not Creole -- signage throughout the central area of the town \parencite{mcneill_bilingualism_2024}.
    Breaux Bridge, on the other hand, was the site of one of the most important documentations of Creole \parencite{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}, and several current participants were associated with this town.
    One of those participants was Errol, a White Cajun who used Creole-marked pronouns.
    Interactions with locals who did not become participants suggest that Errol's linguistic behavior relative to his identity was not unusual.
    Other potentially fruitful towns to examine would be Henderson and Cecilia, although these would perhaps be more difficult to study.
    For Cecilia, the difficulty would be that there is a lack of obvious public gathering locations where participants may be found.
    Henderson has popular public gathering locations in the restaurant Pat's Fisherman Wharf and the adjoining dance hall the Atchafalaya Club.
    However, my own visits to the town and interactions with the people suggest that the people there are particularly wary of strangers asking about their language, and Henderson also has a relatively low Black population relative to the other towns mentioned according to census records.

    Any further variationist analyses of F\&C should also aim to include a full range of ages despite the growing difficulty in doing so.
    \textcite{rottet_language_1995} was able to use a middle age range (\foreign{i.e.}, 30-54) as a bin, and while \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009} chose to focus on speakers over 60 due in part to difficulties in finding younger Cajun speakers, she did report finding Indians in their 30s who only learned English once they began school (p.~65).
    However, the present study had a noticeable gap in the middle age ranges despite making no attempts to prioritize older individuals.
    There are two possible reasons for this.
    There may be a lack of speakers in that age range due to how the valuation of F\&C has shifted back and forth over time.
    A very common personal story for those under 40 or even under 50 is recalling how their grandparents spoke F\&C but not their parents, a story that is even applicable to myself.
    The younger people that I did find who spoke F\&C were either Black, products of immersion schooling, or particularly motivated individuals who had taken it upon themselves to teach themselves the language.
    For these last two categories, \textcite{gudmestad_variationist_2022}, in agreement with \textcite{kasstan_emergent_2019}, suggested that researchers should look at the speech of speakers who have learned French through revitalization programs, so-called new speakers (p.~21), a view that is also shared here.
    The other possible explanation is that those in the middle age ranges are likely to be busier than older folks.
    This means they may have less time available, be less willing to share what time they do have available, and/or are not spending as much time in public locations or at public events where they might be recruited for participation.

    On the social network front, there is a dearth of such data for Louisiana, especially social network data that codes for local social categories.
    Part of the aim of this study was to begin the process of filling in this gap in information.
    The social network data presented here gives a consistent image of Creoles primarily associating with other Creoles and almost never with Cajuns and of Cajuns primarily associating with other Cajuns and only rarely taking on Creoles as acquaintences.
    This is valuable information but not yet generalizable, suggesting the need for social network data collection to continue.
    
    Social network data collection is difficult to get right, however, and in the case of collecting whole network data, it is very time consuming.
    There are indications here, though, that speakers of F\&C, especially Cajuns, have relatively short social distances between them.
    The continued collection of this data can provide a much better picture of those distances as the whole network comes to be elaborated.
    Having such data would allow for studies focusing in on the connections between social structure and racialization by analyzing various forms of segregation in relation to social networks.
    For instance, homophily in personal networks is an unsurprising find when dealing with racialized groups, but it can occur for a number of other reasons in the absence of racialization, whereas segregation is perhaps more related to the sort of conflict that underlies racialization \parencite[p.~346]{gans_racialization_2017}.
    It will be important to further unravel this portion of the analysis to better establish and understand racialization in Louisiana.

    Beyond research, though, social network data may be informative for how to approach F\&C revitalization efforts.
    It is common for F\&C speakers to report having no one with whom to speak the language, but even the very fact that participants can be successfully recruited for studies such as this one is evidence that there are often F\&C speakers hiding in plain sight.
    Knowing more about this can provide avenues to target when attempting to put F\&C speakers into greater contact with each other.
    Network data is also effective for uncovering where languages are being passed along, whether to children, grandchildren, or to any other connection.
    Such knowledge is essential for building effective revitalization programs, especially when resources for such programs are as limited as they are in Louisiana and so cannot be wasted on ineffective initiatives.
  
    Finally, the discourse data presented here was particularly rich and was again consistent with the racialization of Creoles and Cajuns.
    In terms of boundary marking, boundary policing, and boundary maintenance, most of what was uncovered here was marking due to the nature of the data collection procedure generally involving discourse with the researcher only.
    In that marking, Cajuns were treated as a default, White/European group whose Whiteness/Europeanness did not need to be explicitly laid out, whereas Creoles were more explicitly characterized in essentialist terms of blood and color.
    What could still be added to this picture is discourse showing how these boundaries are policed and maintained, which would require various types of group interviews.
    
    \textcite{wendte_tale_2020} did not actively attempt to conduct such group interviews, but just as it happened from time to time in the present study, he did conduct some.
    One of those group interviews provided a striking example of just how valuable this sort of data can be.
    Two of the three participants in the exchange were reported as identifying as White Cajuns except when they were in the presence of the third participant, a Black friend, at which point they identified as Creoles.
    This Black friend, however, while in their company, defined Cajuns as Frenchmen instead of White and then claimed to not know her own identity when asked.
    Instead, she looked to the other two for permission to identify as Creole and denied that she could be Cajun, as well, like her friends could (pp.~293-294).
    Rare in the literature are such extreme instances of boundary policing and maintenance that speak to the clear hierarachy between Creoles and Cajuns in the racialized social system of Louisiana, but their ontological rarity is highly doubtful.
    
    The current study has added a great deal to the understanding of Creoles and Cajuns as racialized groups.
    How their status as such is reproduced over time through boundary policing and maintenance is still to be uncovered, along with the full extent of their social networks and the role of location and age in how F\&C is spoken by locals.